                     <forename>Jae</forename>
                     <roleName>Research Assistant</roleName>
                  </persName>
                  <note resp="#hjb">Hadleigh Jae Bills is a student at Penn State Behrend. She is
                     working towards a B.A. in Digital Media, Arts, and Technology.</note>
               </person>
            </listPerson>
         </div>
         <div type="voynich_team">
            <listPerson>
               <person xml:id="eds">
                  <persName>
                     <surname>Sherwood</surname>
                     <forename>Edith</forename>
                     <roleName>Decoder</roleName>
                  </persName>
                  <note resp="#hjb">Author of many articles about the manuscript. Wrote an article
                     on decoding the plants.</note>
                  <note>
                     <ptr target="https://voynichlanguage.fandom.com/wiki/Edith_Sherwood"/>
                  </note>
               </person>
               <person xml:id="hth">
                  <persName>
                     <surname>Holm</surname>
                     <forename>Herman</forename>
                     <forename>Theodor</forename>
                     <roleName>Botanist</roleName>
                  </persName>
                  <note resp="#hjb">A botanist who built his career around studying plant life
                     across places like Greenland, Colorado, and the United States. After
                     immigrating to the U.S., he worked as a botanist for institutions like the
                     United States National Museum and the Department of Agriculture, continuing his
                     focus on plant research. He later earned his doctorate from the Catholic
                     University of America and eventually returned there as a professor of
                     biological research.</note>
                  <note>
                     <ptr target="https://findingaids.lib.catholic.edu/repositories/2/resources/176"/>
                     <ptr
                        target="https://findingaids.lib.catholic.edu/repositories/2/resources/176/collection_organization"
                     />
                  </note>
               </person>
               <person xml:id="rzn">
                  <persName>
                     <surname>Zandbergen</surname>
                     <forename>René</forename>
                     <roleName>Researcher</roleName>
                  </persName>
                  <note resp="#hjb">René Zandbergen, originally a satellite navigation engineer for
                     the European Space Agency, became deeply involved in studying the mysterious
                     Voynich Manuscript on the side. Starting in 1995, he joined an online community
                     focused on analyzing the manuscript’s strange and unreadable text. Over time,
                     he helped develop a standard way to transcribe its characters and created one
                     of the most widely used websites dedicated to researching the
                     manuscript.</note>
                  <note>
                     <ptr target="https://independent.academia.edu/RenéZandbergen"/>
                  </note>
               </person>
               <person xml:id="tcp">
                  <persName>
                     <surname>Petersen</surname>
                     <forename>Theodore</forename>
                     <roleName>Transcriptor</roleName>
                  </persName>
                  <note resp="#hjb">Fr. Petersen first made a full photocopy of the Voynich
                     manuscript’s photostats, then created a handwritten copy of the manuscript,
                     consulting the original in a New York safe deposit vault when passages were
                     especially difficult. He finished this work in 1944, adding notes on possible
                     meanings, marking unusual character sequences, highlighting common words, and
                     suggesting plant identifications in the herbal section based in part on Holm
                     and O’Neill’s research. He also compiled a complete concordance of the
                     manuscript’s words, though the writer had not seen it. Petersen remained a
                     leading Voynich expert until his death, after which his papers were passed to
                     Friedman, inventoried by Tiltman, and eventually housed at the George C.
                     Marshall Library and Archives in Lexington, Virginia.</note>
                  <note>
                     <ptr target="https://www.voynich.nu/solvers.html#petersen"/>
                  </note>
               </person>
            </listPerson>
